Z||000000||85 gt fiero||10-13-2001||07:00 PM||||I just got back from the barn, ver diasapointed i was going to change the plugs and got the back three off but the front three, cant even see them, do you have to get under the car and change them, also how hard is it to change the alternator, and oh yah one more can i take off the manifold(the part that says fiero on it) and take it to school and buff it on a buffing whell.  Randy        any help appreciated <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||JetroGT||10-13-2001||08:47 PM||||You can get the front plugs from the top. I use a swivel socket and a short extention. I can't see the plugs so I just do it by feel.<P>If you take the plenum off you have to replace the plenum gaskets and the throttle gasket. The shop manual will show you the correct torque sequence to put it back on.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/NewSigt.jpg">  <BR>I work where all the missing paperclips go.<BR>Wanted: 86/87 Red GT, auto, pw, pl, pm, rear defrost,pt, Gray int, Spoiler. May be willing to trade the blue car.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||10-13-2001||08:52 PM||||I find it easiest to take the rear decklid off to change them. Just use whiteout to mark the position of the hinges and take the lid off. Its alot easier then it sounds or looks. I do it all the time now.||||reg||1
Z||000003||85 gt fiero||10-14-2001||12:53 PM||||Were going to the top.! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000004||California Kid||10-14-2001||01:02 PM||||The guys who posted responses already have covered this issue. There is no magic that one can perform on this. You either use a short swivel extension with brail method (feel only), or take the deck lid off so you can kinda see what you're doing. Only other option when it comes to plug changes is to have someone else do it. Note: Make sure you use a real good plug, so you don't have to do this again soon.<BR>Sorry, forgot you asked a couple more questions. Alternator: No big deal, refer to Hayes Manual. Plenum re-finish: If all you're concerned about is the top alum finish, just use some emery sand paper (start with 600, then finer, block sand using straight side to side movement). then cover engine with thin plastic drop cloth, except for area to be clear coated. Use spray clear coat used for top coat paints, it lasts a long time. If you can find a high temp clear coat, use that.<p>[This message has been edited by California Kid (edited 10-14-2001).]||||reg||1
